    "preview": "Hello again, family and friends! Today is all about Okayama, Japan. The internet told me that the city is…",
    "body": "<div><div><div>Hello again, family and friends!<br /><br />Today is all about Okayama, Japan. The internet told me that the city is super uninteresting and that I should just stay on the train. Let me be the first to say that the internet is wrong. I fell in love with the city and spent several days there. Don&#39;t be fooled by the name—Okayama is far better than just okay! 💯</div><img src=\"images/part-2-01.webp\" alt=\"IMG_7228.jpeg\" width=\"450\" height=\"542\" /><br /><div>The number one attraction in Okayama (even the internet conceded that it&#39;s worth a quick glance) is the Kōraku-en, one of the Three Great Gardens of Japan.<br /><img src=\"images/part-2-06.webp\" alt=\"IMG_7056.png\" width=\"542\" height=\"407\" /></div><div>They were raising some kind of bird there.<br /><img src=\"images/part-2-07.webp\" alt=\"2C63C533-E304-43CA-B3BF-41CB97E16C99_1_105_c.jpeg\" width=\"542\" height=\"407\" /><br />Okayama Castle is on the other side of the Asahi River from the Garden. In 2006, Okayama Castle was officially <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/100_Fine_Castles_of_Japan\">designated</a> as a &quot;Fine Castle&quot; by the Japanese Castle Association. Just fine? Why does everyone think it&#39;s okay to denigrate Okayama? I thought the castle was really good. It has a compelling color scheme!<br /></div><img src=\"images/part-2-02.webp\" alt=\"IMG_7062.png\" width=\"542\" height=\"407\" /><br />From the top of the castle, you can see some parts of the city.<br /><img src=\"images/part-2-03.webp\" alt=\"IMG_7065.png\" width=\"407\" height=\"542\" /><br />I was curious about that building in the distance (the one touching the nose of the roof in the picture above). It turned out to be a very pretty brutalist building belonging to the municipal government.<br /><img src=\"images/part-2-04.webp\" alt=\"IMG_7068.png\" width=\"542\" height=\"407\" /><br />The sculptural &quot;O&quot; is probably for &quot;Okayama.&quot;<br /><img src=\"images/part-2-05.webp\" alt=\"IMG_7070.png\" width=\"542\" height=\"407\" /><br />Okayama is home to the Orient Museum. It houses some of Japan&#39;s most interesting artifacts from the Near East. I take my work as a newsletter writer seriously so I made sure to document some of the most compelling pieces:<br /><img src=\"images/part-2-09.webp\" alt=\"IMG_7044.png\" width=\"407\" height=\"542\" /><br /><i>^ some old shoes</i><br /><img src=\"images/part-2-10.webp\" alt=\"IMG_7045.png\" width=\"407\" height=\"542\" /><br /><i>^ a floating rubber duck sporting an Egyptian striped headcloth</i><br /><br />At night I took a stroll down a lovely boulevard with a canal and a park in the median. There were restaurants and late-night cafes and bars on both sides. There was like a San Antonio vibe.<br /><img src=\"images/part-2-11.webp\" alt=\"IMG_7172.png\" width=\"407\" height=\"542\" /><br />It was along this boulevard that I found a supermarket selling famous Okayama white peaches. Very much like shoes, Okayama white peaches are exclusively sold in pairs. I wanted to eat just one at first: the plan was to give the second one to a stranger to make a new friend, but I ended up eating both peaches because of how juicy and sweet they were. I forgot I was allergic to peaches!<br /><img src=\"images/part-2-12.webp\" alt=\"image.png\" width=\"542\" height=\"361\" /><br />I forgot to take a picture of my own Okayama peaches, so here&#39;s a stock picture of a person presumably placidly picking prized peaches.<br /><br />In all, Okayama surpasses its nominative constraints. If I were to come up with a more fitting name, I&#39;d call the city Greatama. Join me next time as I take the Marine Liner across the Seto Sea to Takamatsu, Shikoku, where great things are happening.<br /><br /><u>QotD</u><br />Which would you like to see more of in future installments: food, architecture, nature, art, trenchant cross-cultural insights or something else? Reply below.<br /><br /></div><div><u>P.S.</u></div><div>Thank you all for your emphatic support w/r/t Part 1. I would not have been able to make Part 2 without you! I forgot to mention last time but thank you to Celeste whose daily poetry newsletter inspired me to make this one. <br /><br />Responding to the open question in Part 1, Elyas mentioned a fourth kind of structure—the Japanese apartment building with outside staircases. A classic!<br /><img src=\"images/part-2-13.webp\" alt=\"Screenshot 2026-07-05 at 1.34.17 AM.png\" width=\"246\" height=\"324\" /><br /><br />--<br /><br /><img src=\"images/part-2-08.webp\" alt=\"IMG_7023.png\" width=\"214\" height=\"160\" /><br /><i>surprise set meal (under $15)</i><br /><br /><span>-- <br /></span><br /><div><div><div><b>Noah Lee</b><br /><span><a href=\"mailto:noahdlee519@gmail.com\">noahdlee519@gmail.com</a><br /></span><span>646-634-5484</span></div></div></div></div>\n</div>"
