    "body": "<div><span>Hello all! Welcome to a series of travel updates that I will be making for the next month or two. I’ll be sharing photos from my trips to Japan, Korea, and Czechia, along with any interesting thoughts I have along the way. Thank you to everyone for supporting my travels—it’s been a very meaningful trip so far and I’m eager to share more!</span><div><br /><span>We begin in Himeji, Japan. Every Japanese city worth its salt has a castle district. Himeji has one of the oldest, grandest, and best preserved castles in all of Japan. It&#39;s a UNESCO World Heritage Site and an official National treasure.</span><br /></div><img src=\"images/part-1-16.webp\" alt=\"IMG_7180.png\" width=\"336\" height=\"444\" /><br /><div><span>Himeji Castle during the day:</span><br /><img src=\"images/part-1-01.webp\" alt=\"IMG_6959.png\" width=\"333\" height=\"444\" /><br /><span>Himeji Castle at night:</span><br /><img src=\"images/part-1-02.webp\" alt=\"IMG_7001.png\" width=\"333\" height=\"444\" /><br /><span>I especially enjoyed navigating across the castle&#39;s gardens and canals. I saw some very healthy looking koi fish which was a good sign.</span><br /><img src=\"images/part-1-03.webp\" alt=\"IMG_6967.png\" width=\"333\" height=\"444\" /><br /><img src=\"images/part-1-04.webp\" alt=\"IMG_6980.png\" width=\"444\" height=\"333\" /><br /><img src=\"images/part-1-05.webp\" alt=\"IMG_6979.png\" width=\"333\" height=\"444\" /><br /><span>In Japan, lots of organizations and institutions have their own cartoon mascot ambassadors. I&#39;ve seen everything from companies, museums, prefectures (sort of like states), international expositions, etc. with mascots. I think Himeji Castle has a very compelling mascot character.</span><br /><img src=\"images/part-1-06.webp\" alt=\"IMG_7036.png\" width=\"444\" height=\"333\" /><br /><span>Naturally, I&#39;m sharing food pictures. The sushi in Japan has been very cheap and delicious. The yen is at a 40-year low against the dollar. I got this platter for about ten dollars.</span><br /><img src=\"images/part-1-07.webp\" alt=\"IMG_6987.png\" width=\"333\" height=\"444\" /><br /><span>Night walks in Japan are a lot of fun. It’s generally very safe. However, you do have to watch out for monkeys in Himeji. There was a sign saying that monkeys may be on the loose from the local zoo. I had this in the back of my mind when walking up this very long staircase in the woods. (Okay, the monkeys were really in the front of my mind).</span><br /><img src=\"images/part-1-08.webp\" alt=\"IMG_7008.png\" width=\"333\" height=\"444\" /><br /><span>Thankfully, no monkeys bothered me on my ascent—only a friendly stray cat came over to say hello. I was greeted by a beautiful view at the top.</span><br /><img src=\"images/part-1-09.webp\" alt=\"IMG_7004.png\" width=\"444\" height=\"333\" /><br /><span>The architect Tadao Ando has buildings all over Japan. You can recognize his style because of all the concrete. This is the Himeji Literature Museum.</span><br /><img src=\"images/part-1-10.webp\" alt=\"IMG_7017.png\" width=\"333\" height=\"444\" /><br /><span>You can see the reflection of Himeji Castle in the windows.</span><br /><img src=\"images/part-1-11.webp\" alt=\"IMG_7010.png\" width=\"444\" height=\"333\" /><br /><span>There was a cozy bar on the second floor of this little building which looked like the perfect place to round off the night.</span><br /><img src=\"images/part-1-12.webp\" alt=\"IMG_6988.png\" width=\"444\" height=\"333\" /><br /><span>A prim and proper gentleman with impressive facial hair tended the bar. I ordered the house snacks and a drink called the &quot;El Diabro.&quot;</span><br /><img src=\"images/part-1-13.webp\" alt=\"IMG_7019.png\" width=\"333\" height=\"444\" /><br /><span>I think are basically three types of buildings in Japan. </span><span>First, you have old wood/stone houses, castles, and shrines that have been well preserved. </span><span>Second, you have sleek, ultra-modern hi-tech architecture of glass, concrete, and steel. </span><span>And third, you have dilapidated beige/tan postwar concrete apartment blocks covered in stains. Is there a fourth building type that I&#39;m missing?</span><br /><br /><span>In the morning, I took the Shinkansen (a.k.a. the Bullet Train) west from Himeji to Okayama where I’ll pick up my adventure in the next installment!</span><br /><img src=\"images/part-1-14.webp\" alt=\"IMG_7040.JPG\" width=\"333\" height=\"444\" /><br /><span>Thank you for reading. I’m looking forward to your feedback and comments!<br /></span><br />Sincerely,<br />Noah<br /><br />--<br /><br /><img src=\"images/part-1-15.webp\" alt=\"IMG_7033.png\" width=\"146\" height=\"195\" /><br /><i>surprise ramen</i><br /><span><br />--<br /></span><br /><b>Noah Lee</b><br /><span><a href=\"mailto:noahdlee519@gmail.com\">noahdlee519@gmail.com</a><br /></span><span>646-634-5484</span></div></div>"
